Came across this stack overflow post which helped. https://stackoverflow.com/questions/70296667/how-do-i-connect-to-mongodb-running-in-github-codespaces-using-mongodb-compass

Used ngrok to tunnel it. This was also good learning. Going forward I can use this method on other projects that need a database.  

Learnings: 
1. GridFS class to handle simple objects. 
2. GridFS takes a collection name then instead of fs.files and fs.chunks it will take that name. 
3. I can pass additional parameters to gridFS and it will accept it. These are searchable like a normal mongoDB collection. 
4. GridFSBucket provides additional upload from stream and download to stream methods. 
5. GridFSBucket comes with streamability. 
6. GridFS has a get_version that returns a specific version using the -1 numbering, it filters the files based on the name and then returns the nth from the last version. 
7. Using gridFS as a file storage is an accepted solution when you application is very small and you have low to medium traffic. 
    Why? 
    1. Chunks the files creating more overhead and slower performance. 
    2. Chunking makes serving static files inefficient. 